.TH DF 1 "October 7, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
df \- report volume block usage
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B df
.RB [ \-Pk ]
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The historical
.I df
prints the number of free blocks available on a file system.
This maux implementation prints the POSIX
.B \-P
report, always:
.PP
.nf
Filesystem 512-blocks Used Available Capacity Mounted on
/mnt/MAUX 8192 1689 6503 21% /mnt/MAUX
.fi
.PP
There is one row per volume.
Filesystem and Mounted on are both the volume path:
ProDOS has no device name for a volume.
Used is the blocks for all files and Available is Total less Used;
the volume's own boot, directory and bitmap blocks count as used.
Capacity is the used fraction of the total, rounded up.
All three numbers come from one GET_FILE_INFO on the volume directory
(the ProDOS 8 Technical Reference Manual: the total is returned in
aux_type and the blocks for all files in blocks_used).
.PP
With no operand, every volume that is online is listed, in unit order.
Only non-floppy units are queried: a 5.25-inch drive is never polled
to build the list.
An operand names the volume holding it: a path on the volume
("/bin/ls"), a bare volume name ("BASIC"), or a relative path
("bin", "./x"), which is checked to exist and reported against the
volume of the working directory.
The operand "/" lists every volume, like no operand.
.TP
.B \-P
Accepted; the report is always in this form.
.TP
.B \-k
Use units of 1024 bytes.
Total and Used are halved, rounding up; Available is their difference.
Capacity is unchanged.
A block is 512 bytes, so the default unit is the block.
.SH FILES
/usr/man/DF  this page
.SH "SEE ALSO"
du(1), licenses(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 0 if every operand was reported, 1 if any could not be
accessed ("df: path: cannot access"), 2 for a usage error.
.SH BUGS
There are no other Unix df options, and no per-volume device column.
A volume that goes offline between listing and query is reported as
cannot access.
